                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Having played this game, my hands are still vibrating, my ears are ringing from imaginary machine guns, and somewhere in the distance, a military general is staring at a spreadsheet wondering why one helicopter pilot has single-handedly reduced the global construction industry to rubble. This game is a ACTION MOVIE disguised as a video game. You get into a helicopter, fly around, shoot approximately seven million bullets, launch enough rockets to make NASA jealous, and blow up everything that has the audacity to exist within your general vicinity.\n\nThunder Wolves isn't an extraordinary game. It doesn't revolutionize the action genre, offer an incredible variety of missions, or reinvent helicopter combat. But it doesn't need to. It delivers smooth helicopter controls, satisfying shooting, ridiculous explosions, occasional tank-driving sequences, and a straightforward campaign that makes you feel like the star of an absurdly expensive military action movie. Yes, the missions become repetitive. Yes, the campaign can feel longer than its gameplay variety justifies. And yes, by the end, you'll have destroyed enough infrastructure to make rebuilding civilization humanity's next thousand-year project. But IT'S FUN. And sometimes, that's enough. If you enjoy arcade-style military action and want to spend a few hours flying helicopters, firing machine guns, launching rockets, and turning enemy installations into architectural memories, Thunder Wolves is worth a shot. Just don't expect a masterpiece. Expect explosions. Lots and lots of explosions. And maybe keep a therapist on standby for the construction workers. By the time I reached mission 13, I had destroyed so many structures, shot down so many helicopters, and eliminated so many enemies that my brain had essentially turned into a bowl of scrambled eggs. I couldn't tell you exactly how many bullets I'd fired. I couldn't tell you how many rockets I'd launched. I couldn't tell you how many buildings I'd demolished. My brain was still processing explosions long after I stopped playing. I could close my eyes and hear the machine guns. I could look at a perfectly ordinary building and briefly wonder how many rockets it would take to demolish it. I could hear a helicopter flying overhead and instinctively search for the nearest enemy tank. Interestingly, the game sometimes feels more like a mobile game than a traditional PC title. I don't think I've ever played a game where I've shot this many machine-gun rounds, launched this many rockets, and destroyed this much architecture. By mission 13, I was beginning to wonder whether the developers had accidentally given me the nuclear launch codes instead of a helicopter. At this point, the entire world has been reduced to a pre-industrial wasteland. Humanity has collectively returned to the Stone Age because I apparently have a personal vendetta against buildings.",
